{"answer":"No. Taking the nonnegative integer n=40 gives n^2+n+41=40^2+40+41=1681=41^2, which is composite. Thus the universal statement is false.","approaches":[{"description":"Disprove the universal assertion by finding one permitted value of n for which the polynomial is composite.","id":"counterexample","outcome":"candidate","reason":"The explicit evaluation at n=40 factors as 41^2, so it is a decisive counterexample."},{"description":"Attempt to prove primality for all nonnegative integers from the familiar initial prime values of the polynomial.","id":"initial-pattern","outcome":"rejected","reason":"A finite initial pattern cannot establish a universal claim, and n=40 directly contradicts it."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"question_premise","basis_reference":"The question asks: \"Is n^2+n+41 prime for every nonnegative integer n?\"","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"domain-premise","kind":"assumption","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The proposed statement ranges over every nonnegative integer n.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Direct arithmetic in steps evaluate-forty and factor-value.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["domain-premise"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"composite-counterexample","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"At the permitted input n=40, n^2+n+41 is composite.","step_ids":["evaluate-forty","factor-value"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"A universal statement is false when a member of its stated domain fails the asserted property; applied in step refute-universal.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["composite-counterexample"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"statement-false","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The statement that n^2+n+41 is prime for every nonnegative integer n is false.","step_ids":["refute-universal"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"evaluate-forty","justification":"Substitution and integer arithmetic give 40^2+40+41=1600+40+41=1681.","statement":"For n=40, n^2+n+41=1681."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["evaluate-forty"],"id":"factor-value","justification":"1681=41·41, and 41 is an integer strictly between 1 and 1681.","statement":"The value 1681 is composite."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["factor-value"],"id":"refute-universal","justification":"By domain-premise, 40 is among the quantified nonnegative integers. The composite value from factor-value contradicts the asserted primality at that input.","statement":"The single value n=40 refutes the universal assertion."}],"question_status":"refuted","tool_requests":[]}
